name: bytes
boss: Void
flavor: Nothing you deleted is as gone as you think.
brief: Text tools stop at the first byte that is not text. hexdump and od show
brief: you what is really there. dd moves raw blocks with no interpretation at
brief: all, which is why it can copy a whole disk -- and why a typo in its
brief: arguments is the most expensive typo on this system.
brief:
brief: dd names its arguments with = rather than flags: if= in, of= out, bs=
brief: block size, count= how many blocks.
---
q: %show% @bin@ as hex bytes with the printable characters beside them
a: hexdump -C @bin@
out: \|
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -C
hint: C is the canonical format, and the only one worth memorising
---
q: {dump only the first 32 bytes of @bin@|show just the first 32 bytes of @bin@, nothing past that|limit the dump of @bin@ to its first 32 bytes}
a: hexdump -C -n 32 @bin@
out: \|
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -n
hint: -n caps how many bytes hexdump reads, from the start
---
q: {dump @bin@ starting 8 bytes in, skipping the header|skip the first 8 bytes of @bin@ and dump what follows|show @bin@ from byte 8 onward, past its header}
a: hexdump -C -s 8 @bin@
out: \|
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -s
hint: -s skips that many bytes before the dump begins
---
q: {dump @txt@ in octal with od, the older tool that does the same job|use od, hexdump's older cousin, to dump @txt@ in octal|show @txt@ in octal using od rather than hexdump}
a: od @txt@
out: [0-7]
ref: od
teach: od
hint: od's default is octal words -- no flags needed here
---
q: {copy @txt@ to copy.txt using dd|use dd to copy @txt@ into copy.txt|make a copy of @txt@ named copy.txt with dd}
a: dd if=@txt@ of=copy.txt
state: exists:copy.txt
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: if= is the source, of= is the destination -- no other flags needed
---
q: {copy the first 512 bytes of @log@ into head.bin|use dd to copy just the first 512 bytes of @log@ into head.bin|pull the first 512 bytes off @log@ and write them to head.bin}
a: dd if=@log@ of=head.bin bs=512 count=1
state: exists:head.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: bs= sets the block size, count= how many blocks to copy
---
q: {read 16 bytes from @log@ starting at byte 32|starting 32 bytes into @log@, read the next 16 bytes|skip the first 32 bytes of @log@ and read 16 bytes from there}
a: dd if=@log@ bs=1 skip=32 count=16
out: .
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: skip= moves the read start point; bs=1 makes skip/count mean bytes
---
q: %make% a 1 kilobyte file of zero bytes
a: dd if=/dev/zero of=zero.bin bs=1k count=1
state: exists:zero.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: /dev/zero is an endless stream of zero bytes; bs=/count= cap it
---
q: %make% 32 bytes of random data
a: dd if=/dev/urandom of=rand.bin bs=32 count=1
state: exists:rand.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: /dev/urandom is the random byte source; bs= sets how many bytes total
---
q: {copy @txt@ with dd, showing no progress or summary at all|use dd to copy @txt@ while suppressing all progress and summary output|copy @txt@ with dd as quietly as possible, with no summary line}
a: dd if=@txt@ of=copy.txt status=none
state: exists:copy.txt
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: status= controls dd's progress/summary output -- none silences it
---
q: {split @log@ into pieces of 2 lines each|break @log@ into pieces of 2 lines apiece|use split to cut @log@ into 2-line chunks}
a: split -l 2 @log@
state: exists:xaa
ref: split
teach: split -l
hint: -l counts lines per output piece, split's default unit
---
q: {split @log@ into pieces of 100 bytes each|break @log@ into 100-byte pieces|use split to cut @log@ into chunks of 100 bytes}
a: split -b 100 @log@
state: exists:xaa
ref: split
teach: split -b
hint: -b counts bytes per piece instead of lines
---
q: {open @bin@ in a hex editor to change bytes in place, not just read them|open @bin@ in something that lets you edit bytes, not just view them|unlike hexdump, open @bin@ in a tool that can write bytes back}
a: hexedit @bin@
why: interactive
ref: hexedit
teach: hexedit
hint: hexdump shows you bytes; this is how you change them
